A agent is caller on your platform,
treat it in that way, and most of

28
00:01:50,956 --> 00:01:52,604
the production problems get smaller.

29
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:56,984
So demos are easy, but
production is not at all

30
00:01:57,984 --> 00:02:02,409
Yeah, so that gap is where
most agent, projects stall.

31
00:02:02,482 --> 00:02:04,421
In a demo, you have one happy path.

32
00:02:04,976 --> 00:02:07,954
The data is clean, a human
is watching every step, and

33
00:02:07,955 --> 00:02:09,570
if it fails, nothing breaks.

34
00:02:09,899 --> 00:02:13,028
You fix the prompt, you run it
again, and everyone moves on.

35
00:02:13,441 --> 00:02:16,034
Production is different on
every one of those points.

36
00:02:16,062 --> 00:02:19,613
The data is messy, and it changes
while the agent is working.

37
00:02:20,045 --> 00:02:22,768
Runs fail in the middle, not at the start.

38
00:02:23,432 --> 00:02:27,595
There is no one watching at 2:00 in
the morning, and the data is real,

39
00:02:27,765 --> 00:02:30,042
so the consequences are also real.

40
00:02:30,535 --> 00:02:34,128
Here is the line I want you
to keep from this slide.

41
00:02:34,144 --> 00:02:36,507
The gap is not the model, it's control.

42
00:02:37,614 --> 00:02:42,464
Who the agent is, what it may touch,
and what happens when it goes wrong.

43
00:02:42,820 --> 00:02:45,480
A better model does not
fix any of these problems.

44
00:02:46,231 --> 00:02:49,315
You can swap the model
and keep the same risk.

45
00:02:49,452 --> 00:02:55,068
I have seen teams spend months on model
quality while the agent still ran under

46
00:02:55,073 --> 00:02:59,976
a shared account with broader access
and no clear record of what it did.

47
00:03:00,042 --> 00:03:03,538
That's not a model problem, that's
a pro- pro-- platform, problem.

48
00:03:03,982 --> 00:03:05,765
So the talk is about control.

49
00:03:05,774 --> 00:03:10,166
No control as paperwork,
control as design, identity,

50
00:03:10,167 --> 00:03:12,246
scope and a gate and a record.

51
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:15,737
Get those right and the
model can improve on its own.

52
00:03:17,054 --> 00:03:18,273
Keep them, a demo.

53
00:03:18,306 --> 00:03:22,541
By the way, demos are how you
learn what an agent can do.

54
00:03:22,552 --> 00:03:26,618
Just do not mistake a good
demo for permission to ship

55
00:03:27,618 --> 00:03:30,610
The word "a regulated
enterprise" changes everything.

56
00:03:30,871 --> 00:03:33,816
Why does the word "regulated"
change this so much?

57
00:03:35,219 --> 00:03:39,264
A sensitive data agents work
on data you cannot leak, guess,

58
00:03:39,650 --> 00:03:41,777
or expose to the wrong caller.

59
00:03:42,269 --> 00:03:45,366
So access is not convenient
setting, it is the design.

60
00:03:46,490 --> 00:03:48,271
Second, approval matters.

61
00:03:48,272 --> 00:03:53,930
Some actions need a human approvals,
not a log entry after the fact.

62
00:03:54,409 --> 00:03:58,792
If the action changes a record, moves
work forward or cannot be undone.

63
00:03:59,489 --> 00:04:01,648
Someone has to agree before it happens.

64
00:04:01,693 --> 00:04:06,471
A log that says, "It already
happened," is not an approval.

65
00:04:06,570 --> 00:04:08,504
Third, audit is not an optional.

66
00:04:08,601 --> 00:04:13,789
You must be able to show who did
it, when, and under which approval.

67
00:04:14,503 --> 00:04:15,912
Not roughly, exactly.

68
00:04:15,920 --> 00:04:20,854
If you cannot show it, you cannot defend
that run, and in regulator setting, that

69
00:04:20,855 --> 00:04:23,309
means the run should not have happened.

70
00:04:23,353 --> 00:04:24,957
Fourth, someone is accountable.

71
00:04:25,322 --> 00:04:27,412
A named owner should be always there.

72
00:04:28,341 --> 00:04:31,073
"The agent did it," is never an answer.

73
00:04:31,892 --> 00:04:34,607
Agent has no judgment
and no accountability.

74
00:04:34,922 --> 00:04:35,870
A person has.

75
00:04:35,883 --> 00:04:41,502
So every agent needs a person
who name-- named on it.

76
00:04:41,609 --> 00:04:45,003
So none of this is an
anti-agent, it's the opposite.

77
00:04:46,910 --> 00:04:52,116
These four points are very critical
in production and keeps saying yes

78
00:04:52,178 --> 00:04:54,463
after the first hard question arrives.

79
00:04:55,045 --> 00:04:55,348
Exactly

80
00:04:56,348 --> 00:04:58,913
So what we learn the hard way?

81
00:04:59,151 --> 00:05:00,575
What is hard thing here?

82
00:05:00,904 --> 00:05:04,739
Here is the whole talk on
the slide, three lessons.

83
00:05:04,780 --> 00:05:08,007
The rest of the talk is three in detail.

84
00:05:08,013 --> 00:05:12,356
One, first lesson is give
every agent an identity.

85
00:05:12,649 --> 00:05:15,797
Anonymous agents cannot be
scoped, audited, or revoked.

86
00:05:15,846 --> 00:05:17,782
If you cannot name it,
you cannot control it.

87
00:05:18,247 --> 00:05:22,225
Two, gate actions by risk, not by hope.

88
00:05:22,893 --> 00:05:28,200
Low risk runs, high risk waits for
human approval The decision is made

89
00:05:28,215 --> 00:05:33,193
before the action you get outside
the agent, not by the agent itself.

90
00:05:33,423 --> 00:05:36,245
Three, treat evaluation as a release gate.

91
00:05:36,706 --> 00:05:43,235
Agent ships only when it passes the
evaluation, and it keeps passing because

92
00:05:43,271 --> 00:05:46,083
behavior drifts as models and data change.

93
00:05:46,576 --> 00:05:51,834
Keep these three in mind, identity first,
gate, and then proof that it still works.

94
00:05:53,196 --> 00:05:56,237
You do not need a new
platform to use this.

95
00:05:56,327 --> 00:05:59,641
You need the four steps
in the path of every call

96
00:06:00,641 --> 00:06:04,049
The reference pattern, a control
plane for every agent action.

97
00:06:04,286 --> 00:06:09,824
This is reference pattern Think of it as
a control plane for every agent action.

98
00:06:09,832 --> 00:06:10,728
Four steps.

99
00:06:10,978 --> 00:06:12,309
Step one is identity.

100
00:06:12,373 --> 00:06:13,262
Who is calling?

101
00:06:13,830 --> 00:06:17,478
Which-- not which team, not which
application, which agent owned by

102
00:06:17,493 --> 00:06:19,702
whom, running for what purpose?

103
00:06:20,270 --> 00:06:21,837
Step two is scoped grant.

104
00:06:23,330 --> 00:06:26,486
What may it touch and for how long?

105
00:06:26,853 --> 00:06:31,486
The grant is narrow, and it expires
after the work has been done

106
00:06:32,486 --> 00:06:34,581
And step three is policy gate.

107
00:06:35,043 --> 00:06:38,030
Is this action allowed
or does a human decide?

108
00:06:39,513 --> 00:06:43,104
The gate looks at the action and
the data, and it makes a call.

109
00:06:43,270 --> 00:06:45,528
Low risk passes, high
risk stops for human.

110
00:06:46,831 --> 00:06:47,958
Step four is audit.

111
00:06:48,088 --> 00:06:52,359
Record it, immutability, like
which agent, which grant, who

112
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:55,462
is involved, the human, name.

113
00:06:56,595 --> 00:06:59,551
And every tool call and every
data operation passes through the

114
00:06:59,552 --> 00:07:01,946
same four steps, no side doors.

115
00:07:02,432 --> 00:07:05,521
The last part matters more than a diagram.

116
00:07:05,668 --> 00:07:09,340
A pattern with bypass is not a pattern.

117
00:07:10,697 --> 00:07:14,829
If a tool can be called directly, it
will be usually under the pressure

118
00:07:15,829 --> 00:07:21,308
Notice the order of four, actions
and identify and grant before

119
00:07:21,344 --> 00:07:26,041
gate, gate before action, and the
record to return as it happens

120
00:07:27,041 --> 00:07:31,433
Every agent is a caller you
can name, scope, and revoke.

121
00:07:32,974 --> 00:07:36,561
Start with identity because
everything else hangs off it.

122
00:07:36,827 --> 00:07:39,726
One identity per agent, never
a shared service account.

123
00:07:40,741 --> 00:07:43,250
A shared service account
feels easier at the start.

124
00:07:43,301 --> 00:07:43,918
It's not.

125
00:07:45,116 --> 00:07:48,265
When three agents share the account,
your audit trail says the account

126
00:07:48,282 --> 00:07:50,642
did it, and that tells you nothing.

127
00:07:51,272 --> 00:07:55,296
You cannot tell which agent acted,
and you cannot stop one of them

128
00:07:55,315 --> 00:07:56,806
without stopping all of them.

129
00:07:57,931 --> 00:08:01,715
The identity has to carry three things:
the owner, the purpose, and the scope.

130
00:08:02,315 --> 00:08:06,037
Owner, so a named person is accountable.

131
00:08:06,382 --> 00:08:11,845
Purpose, and you can judge whether a
request fits what this agent is for.

132
00:08:11,959 --> 00:08:16,594
Scope, so the platform
knows what it may reach.

133
00:08:17,254 --> 00:08:20,926
And you must be able to revoke the
agent without breaking the rest.

134
00:08:21,074 --> 00:08:23,218
Revoke is the res- test, actually.

135
00:08:23,984 --> 00:08:29,469
If stopping the misbehaving agent means
taking down a shared account and breaking

136
00:08:29,469 --> 00:08:31,181
four other agents, you will hesitate.

137
00:08:31,432 --> 00:08:34,244
Hesitation is how a small
problem runs for hours.

138
00:08:34,881 --> 00:08:36,771
Names matters in a particular way too.

139
00:08:37,528 --> 00:08:41,632
So here is the rule from the
slide, and I mean it literally.

140
00:08:41,650 --> 00:08:44,302
If you cannot name the caller,
you cannot trust the call

141
00:08:45,302 --> 00:08:48,850
Least priv-privilege,
scoped, time bound per task.

142
00:08:49,363 --> 00:08:52,456
Identify, say who is calling.

143
00:08:52,598 --> 00:08:56,382
The grant says what the
caller may do this time.

144
00:08:57,027 --> 00:08:59,889
Three words on the slide,
scoped, timed, per task.

145
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:04,713
Scoped means only the data
and tools this task needs.

146
00:09:05,103 --> 00:09:06,887
Nothing standing, nothing broad.

147
00:09:07,365 --> 00:09:13,460
If the task needs one kind of a record
The grant reaches that kind of a record,

148
00:09:13,610 --> 00:09:18,470
not the store it s-sits in, not the
neighborhood stores, just in case.

149
00:09:19,134 --> 00:09:24,605
Timed means grants expire when the
task ends, not when someone remembers

150
00:09:24,631 --> 00:09:29,749
to clean up Standing across is how a
small error becomes a larger incident.

151
00:09:29,867 --> 00:09:34,259
An agent will stop access, and a
wrong input does not make one mistake.

152
00:09:34,946 --> 00:09:41,957
It's someone notices narrow the reach
and shorten the time, and the same wrong

153
00:09:41,967 --> 00:09:49,641
input is small contained error you can
explain and fix Next The policy gate.

154
00:09:50,311 --> 00:09:55,771
What's the policy and what's the
risk it decides and not the agent?

155
00:09:57,387 --> 00:10:02,980
So this is the slide to show down because
this is where control becomes real.

156
00:10:04,276 --> 00:10:05,941
Risk decides, not the agent.

157
00:10:06,707 --> 00:10:11,901
The gate sorts each action
into three bands: low risk and

158
00:10:12,036 --> 00:10:15,445
reversible, auto-approve, and log it.

159
00:10:15,947 --> 00:10:20,424
Medium risk, approve, but with
tighter scope and a shorter grant.

160
00:10:20,944 --> 00:10:26,310
The action can be processed and
less it may touch, less time it has.

161
00:10:26,730 --> 00:10:29,846
High risk, irreversible
stop and escalate to human.

162
00:10:30,562 --> 00:10:36,691
So the, the three decision points to hold,
and it's a policy you have to follow.

163
00:10:37,562 --> 00:10:39,701
The agent never grades its own homework.

164
00:10:39,735 --> 00:10:43,874
An agent that can approve its own
high-risk action is not a gated.

165
00:10:44,672 --> 00:10:48,821
The risk comes from the action
and the data So write the

166
00:10:48,833 --> 00:10:50,449
bands down before you ship.

167
00:10:50,571 --> 00:10:55,723
If the bands only exist in a reviewer's
head, every reviewer will draw them

168
00:10:55,767 --> 00:11:00,884
in a different place, and you will
find that out during an incident only

169
00:11:01,884 --> 00:11:04,395
Audit everything an agent does.

170
00:11:04,836 --> 00:11:05,218
Everything.

171
00:11:06,218 --> 00:11:09,644
Every tool call and every data
operation recorded immutability.

172
00:11:09,656 --> 00:11:11,076
Four things in each record.

173
00:11:11,115 --> 00:11:14,802
Which agent, which grant,
which policy decision allowed,

174
00:11:15,426 --> 00:11:17,895
and which human approved it.

175
00:11:18,813 --> 00:11:20,077
When a human has in the loop.

176
00:11:21,590 --> 00:11:24,421
Immutability is doing real
work in that sentence.

177
00:11:25,004 --> 00:11:29,760
A record that can be edited after
the fact, a draft, not evidence.

178
00:11:30,170 --> 00:11:35,397
The record has to stand as it was
written at this time of the run.

179
00:11:36,022 --> 00:11:40,094
If you cannot reconstruct a run
later, you do not control it now.

180
00:11:41,764 --> 00:11:43,325
Test your audit the hard way.

181
00:11:43,871 --> 00:11:48,738
Pick an old run at random and
rebuild it from the record alone.

182
00:11:49,569 --> 00:11:50,910
What did the agent do?

183
00:11:51,012 --> 00:11:52,008
In what order?

184
00:11:52,232 --> 00:11:54,028
Under what approval?

185
00:11:54,102 --> 00:11:58,641
If you have to guess it at any step,
the record is not finished, and that

186
00:11:58,643 --> 00:12:05,032
is worth knowing in quite a way rather
than a loud one Keep the record where

187
00:12:05,058 --> 00:12:10,242
the team that owns the agent can
actually read it and audit trial.

188
00:12:10,681 --> 00:12:14,086
Nobody can query a compliance
artifact, not a controller

189
00:12:15,086 --> 00:12:18,635
And Evaluation is promotion
gate, not a report.

190
00:12:19,703 --> 00:12:23,823
Evaluation is where a lot of agent
programs look controlled and are

191
00:12:23,823 --> 00:12:27,033
not, so let me be precise on that.

192
00:12:27,336 --> 00:12:29,662
Evaluation is promotion gate, not report.

193
00:12:30,376 --> 00:12:33,116
An agent ships only after
it passes evaluation.

194
00:12:33,126 --> 00:12:36,760
That rule is enforced by
the platform, not a check.

195
00:12:37,850 --> 00:12:43,261
A failed evaluation blocks promotion the
same way failed test blocks a release.

196
00:12:45,141 --> 00:12:48,472
If a failing result can be
noted, discussed, and shipped

197
00:12:48,528 --> 00:12:51,083
anyway, you do not have a gate.

198
00:12:51,930 --> 00:12:54,962
You have a report that people
read on the way to shipping.

199
00:12:55,606 --> 00:13:00,483
Regression evaluation runs every
change, not just a model changes.

200
00:13:01,235 --> 00:13:05,562
After a change to the model, the
prompt, the tools, or the data access,

201
00:13:05,961 --> 00:13:07,966
each of those can change behavior.

202
00:13:08,371 --> 00:13:10,510
A new tool is a new capability.

203
00:13:10,937 --> 00:13:12,794
Wider data access is a new risk.

204
00:13:13,759 --> 00:13:17,885
One practical point, in a regulated
setting, production traces often cannot be

205
00:13:17,886 --> 00:13:24,988
shared outside the lane that owns the data
And passing once, not passing for good.

206
00:13:25,272 --> 00:13:27,738
Behavior drifts as a
model and data change.

207
00:13:28,348 --> 00:13:32,724
The agent you approved three months ago
is not quite the agent running today,

208
00:13:32,826 --> 00:13:34,623
even if nobody touches this code.

209
00:13:34,988 --> 00:13:38,310
Rerun the gate on a schedule
and on every change.

210
00:13:38,335 --> 00:13:42,149
This is what keeps
passing the result honest

211
00:13:43,149 --> 00:13:44,006
Observability.

212
00:13:44,059 --> 00:13:50,805
Traces of what the agent did and why
Audit tells what a record must hold.

213
00:13:50,867 --> 00:13:56,091
Observability is ab-about whe-whether
you can actually follow a run,

214
00:13:56,304 --> 00:13:59,637
why it happens, and after it ends.

215
00:13:59,789 --> 00:14:00,873
Three questions in order.

216
00:14:01,278 --> 00:14:06,848
First, what it did, each step, each
tool call, each data operation, in

217
00:14:06,850 --> 00:14:08,902
order, not a summary written at the end.

218
00:14:09,884 --> 00:14:12,828
The steps themselves in
the sequence they happened.

219
00:14:12,932 --> 00:14:15,874
Second, what-- why did it do it?

220
00:14:16,310 --> 00:14:19,525
The grant behind the step, the
policy decision that allowed

221
00:14:19,525 --> 00:14:21,377
it, and the inputs it acted.

222
00:14:21,826 --> 00:14:29,309
This is part most traces leave out, and
it is the part you need first as a result.

223
00:14:29,896 --> 00:14:35,867
A trace that shows actions without reasons
tells you what happened and leaves you

224
00:14:35,889 --> 00:14:38,052
guessing about whether it should have.

225
00:14:38,760 --> 00:14:40,877
Third, where did it stop?

226
00:14:40,961 --> 00:14:46,295
The exact step a run passed, failed,
or was escalated, not just it ran.

227
00:14:48,020 --> 00:14:49,653
Not a final status with no location.

228
00:14:49,666 --> 00:14:54,701
If a run stops at step 14, you
need step 14, what it was trying

229
00:14:54,701 --> 00:14:56,702
to do and what the gate decided.

230
00:14:56,754 --> 00:15:01,683
Put together those three run from
black box into something reviewer

231
00:15:01,738 --> 00:15:08,981
can re-read, and a run reviewer
can read as a run you can improve.

232
00:15:10,610 --> 00:15:15,194
So when you review a run yourself, read
the trace the way that reviewer will.

233
00:15:15,861 --> 00:15:22,190
Start at the stop point and walk backwards
to the decision that led there What

234
00:15:22,191 --> 00:15:27,999
actually goes wrong mid-run of an engine?

235
00:15:28,999 --> 00:15:31,675
Because production is
where the pattern ends.

236
00:15:32,965 --> 00:15:33,641
Let's keep.

237
00:15:34,384 --> 00:15:36,712
Four things actually go wrong mid-run.

238
00:15:36,762 --> 00:15:38,703
First one is bad or changed data.

239
00:15:40,362 --> 00:15:45,145
The agent keeps going on inputs that
no longer mean what it thinks they can.

240
00:15:45,614 --> 00:15:49,903
The run looks healthy, the work
is wrong, scoped access limits

241
00:15:49,919 --> 00:15:53,005
how far that work can reach.

242
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:57,933
Second, if tool fails halfway,
half the work is done and half is

243
00:15:57,936 --> 00:16:00,289
not, and no one knows which half.

244
00:16:01,192 --> 00:16:04,474
Take an agent what updates
several records in one run.

245
00:16:04,604 --> 00:16:08,855
If it stops in the middle,
you need the trace to yourself

246
00:16:09,855 --> 00:16:11,387
And which records are changed.

247
00:16:11,421 --> 00:16:16,826
Without it, recovery is guesswork, and
guesswork is in real is its own incident.

248
00:16:17,782 --> 00:16:19,585
Third, loops and retrace.

249
00:16:21,494 --> 00:16:26,322
A stack agent repeats an action,
and each repeat has real cost.

250
00:16:27,800 --> 00:16:31,252
It can write again, call
again, charge effort again.

251
00:16:32,157 --> 00:16:35,162
Step limits exits for this.

252
00:16:35,261 --> 00:16:38,918
An agent that cannot stop itself
must be stopped by the platform.

253
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:42,112
Yeah, platform should stop that agent.

254
00:16:43,210 --> 00:16:49,688
Fourth, blast radius, scoped grants,
step limits, and kill switch.

255
00:16:50,249 --> 00:16:51,696
Keep one bad run small.

256
00:16:52,460 --> 00:16:54,759
None of these make the agent smarter.

257
00:16:54,781 --> 00:16:58,530
They make its mistake
cheaper in production.

258
00:16:58,829 --> 00:17:00,569
That trade is worth taking.

259
00:17:02,553 --> 00:17:04,437
Notice a pattern across all four.

260
00:17:05,687 --> 00:17:08,082
None of them is the model
being unintelligent.

261
00:17:08,097 --> 00:17:10,838
They are ordinary system
failures, and ordinary system

262
00:17:10,840 --> 00:17:13,389
controls are what contain them.

263
00:17:15,238 --> 00:17:19,282
When the human is not there,
how agent should behave?

264
00:17:20,282 --> 00:17:21,914
Also, reviewers sleep.

265
00:17:22,201 --> 00:17:24,331
There are no meetings, there are no leave.

266
00:17:24,890 --> 00:17:26,914
There are leave, for example, reviewers.

267
00:17:26,955 --> 00:17:31,771
If your design quietly assumes a reviewer
is always available, the design will

268
00:17:31,806 --> 00:17:37,658
make its own decision at 2:00 in the
morning, and it will make the wrong one.

269
00:17:37,915 --> 00:17:40,778
So decide in advance and
write it into policy.

270
00:17:41,658 --> 00:17:44,604
High risk work waits.

271
00:17:44,957 --> 00:17:47,740
It does not quietly proceed
because nobody's answered.

272
00:17:49,162 --> 00:17:50,335
Waiting a valid state.

273
00:17:50,437 --> 00:17:54,618
Proceeding without approval is not safe.

274
00:17:54,925 --> 00:17:59,046
Reversible steps can continue
under a pre-approval grant.

275
00:17:59,183 --> 00:18:00,484
That is what the balance.

276
00:18:01,683 --> 00:18:04,634
And the run records exactly
where it paused and why.

277
00:18:05,287 --> 00:18:11,336
When the reviewer returns, they
resume from clear state, not a guess.

278
00:18:12,066 --> 00:18:16,587
What it is done, why it is waiting,
and what decision is needed.

279
00:18:17,342 --> 00:18:20,763
This question, how work should
continue safely when a person

280
00:18:20,766 --> 00:18:24,477
temporarily unavailable, is
an area I'm researching now.

281
00:18:25,018 --> 00:18:27,022
Actually, I will not claim results here.

282
00:18:27,151 --> 00:18:30,323
I will say the production
lesson is already clear.

283
00:18:31,625 --> 00:18:35,949
A pause, a pause, you design is safe.

284
00:18:35,995 --> 00:18:38,000
A pause, you improve is not

285
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:42,046
Roll out each step, gate each step.

286
00:18:43,042 --> 00:18:46,929
How do you roll this out without
betting the platform on the first run?

287
00:18:47,684 --> 00:18:52,070
Pilot first, small scope,
close watching, one owner only.

288
00:18:52,630 --> 00:18:54,673
You are not proving that agent is perfect.

289
00:18:54,693 --> 00:18:58,493
You are proving that controls
working and the identity grant

290
00:18:58,805 --> 00:19:00,906
the gate and the record also.

291
00:19:01,587 --> 00:19:05,500
Then shadow, the agent runs
alongside the real process and its

292
00:19:05,501 --> 00:19:07,941
actions are compared, not executed.

293
00:19:08,966 --> 00:19:13,950
You learn where it agrees with the
current process and where it differs

294
00:19:14,767 --> 00:19:16,703
with no risk to the real work.

295
00:19:17,617 --> 00:19:19,638
The differences are interesting part.

296
00:19:20,224 --> 00:19:24,159
Review them before the agent
touches anything in production.

297
00:19:24,701 --> 00:19:32,136
Then scope to production and expand live
but narrow, wider only after evolution

298
00:19:32,570 --> 00:19:34,754
and audit hold up in production.

299
00:19:34,865 --> 00:19:36,321
One real runs over time.

300
00:19:36,828 --> 00:19:40,093
Do not skip shadow because
the pilot went well.

301
00:19:40,449 --> 00:19:41,749
That is a very big mistake.

302
00:19:42,427 --> 00:19:45,310
A pilot is watched closely by definition.

303
00:19:45,383 --> 00:19:50,809
Shadow is where you find out what
the agent does when the work is

304
00:19:50,822 --> 00:19:53,955
real and nobody is leaning over it.

305
00:19:54,589 --> 00:20:01,171
Each move is a decision with evidence
behind it, not a gate or date on a plan.

306
00:20:03,147 --> 00:20:05,146
Checklist before any agent ships

307
00:20:06,146 --> 00:20:10,549
So let's see ten items.

308
00:20:10,615 --> 00:20:12,004
I will walk through each of them.

309
00:20:12,735 --> 00:20:14,834
Group one is identity and access.

310
00:20:14,868 --> 00:20:17,554
One identity per agent with a named owner.

311
00:20:18,827 --> 00:20:25,332
Grants scoped to the task and time-bound,
and no standing access to sensitive data.

312
00:20:26,649 --> 00:20:31,201
If there are three are
not true, stop here.

313
00:20:31,430 --> 00:20:35,372
The rest cannot com-compensate
for broad permanent access.

314
00:20:35,817 --> 00:20:37,863
Group two is the gate and the record.

315
00:20:38,030 --> 00:20:42,795
The policy gate, it's outside
the agent, and it is risk-based.

316
00:20:43,218 --> 00:20:46,547
High-risk actions escalate
to human, and every tool call

317
00:20:46,601 --> 00:20:48,163
and data operation is audited.

318
00:20:48,993 --> 00:20:52,917
This is where the group that decides
what happens on that bad day.

319
00:20:53,421 --> 00:20:55,484
So test it before the bad day.

320
00:20:55,516 --> 00:20:57,545
Escalate on purpose in a pilot.

321
00:20:57,799 --> 00:20:58,848
Read the audit record.

322
00:21:00,100 --> 00:21:01,782
Group three is proof and limits.

323
00:21:01,805 --> 00:21:06,744
Evaluation has passed, and it
blocks promotion when it fails.

324
00:21:07,386 --> 00:21:09,896
Traces show what agent did and why.

325
00:21:10,037 --> 00:21:14,151
Step limits, blast radius controls,
and a kill switch are in place.

326
00:21:14,670 --> 00:21:17,661
And there is a defined pause
when no reviewer is available.

327
00:21:19,391 --> 00:21:21,758
Ten items, none of them is exotic.

328
00:21:22,051 --> 00:21:25,154
Together they are difference
between an agent you can defend

329
00:21:25,238 --> 00:21:27,699
and agent you can only describe.

330
00:21:28,482 --> 00:21:33,889
Run this list before any agent ships,
and run it again after any change to

331
00:21:33,891 --> 00:21:36,453
the model, the tools, and the data

332
00:21:37,453 --> 00:21:39,099
And finally, thank you everyone.

333
00:21:39,174 --> 00:21:42,772
I will leave you with a
line on the closing slide.

334
00:21:44,270 --> 00:21:47,331
So control the agent like
you control any caller.

335
00:21:47,577 --> 00:21:49,798
Name it, scope it, and gate it, record it.

336
00:21:50,638 --> 00:21:54,728
Do these four things, and you can
ship agents in a regulated enterprise

337
00:21:54,735 --> 00:21:56,454
without hoping nothing goes wrong.

338
00:21:57,334 --> 00:21:59,082
Things will go wrong.

339
00:21:59,212 --> 00:22:04,341
Your job is to make sure a wrong run
is small, visible, and explainable.

340
00:22:05,687 --> 00:22:06,639
Thank you for your time.

341
00:22:06,665 --> 00:22:11,254
Thank you for Conf42
for having me, MLOps 2026.

342
00:22:11,808 --> 00:22:13,543
Please take the checklist with you.

343
00:22:13,559 --> 00:22:17,979
It is a part of this talk I
mostly want to see you use too.

344
00:22:18,561 --> 00:22:22,480
You can find me on LinkedIn and on GitHub.

345
00:22:23,724 --> 00:22:29,272
I would value hearing how you teams handle
this policy gate and the evaluation gate.

346
00:22:29,857 --> 00:22:34,934
Those are the two places where
every organization I talk to

347
00:22:34,936 --> 00:22:36,571
has made a different call.

348
00:22:36,749 --> 00:22:39,711
Thank you, and good luck
shipping the agents.
